{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"c664666e2ac3bca7","type":"group","x":1040,"y":-5900,"width":5520,"height":3240,"color":"2","label":"Classes"},
		{"id":"9462d273d28b33f1","type":"group","x":-2040,"y":-4500,"width":2960,"height":1840,"color":"#ae6bff","label":"Code Structure"},
		{"id":"175aa85f3cca83bc","type":"group","x":-2000,"y":-2100,"width":3920,"height":1373,"label":"Relevant Pages"},
		{"id":"e2e57bdeb04ad18c","type":"text","text":"# `HTTP_Message`\nAbstract class denoting an HTTP Message, Request or Response.\n## Types\n\n## Member Functions\n- `body`: Get a reference to the body string\n- `append_body`: Append some data to the body string (useful for parsing and response building by chunks)?\n- `header(string)`: Get a header according to the query string. Look-up by header name.\n- `set_header(string, string)`: Add a new header to the request.\n- `append_body(string)`: Add more bytes to the body of the request.\n## Data Members\n- Two integers, each one denoting the major and minor HTTP version.\n- A multimap of headers, mapping a string to a string to represent all HTTP headers and their associated values.\n- A string (or other, simpler type?) containing the message body, if present. This will have a fixed size depending on the `Content-Length` HTTP header. Whether this is filled with actual data or not is dependent on the request method and/or http headers. Responses always have a body.","x":2223,"y":-5840,"width":737,"height":740},
		{"id":"35d69298a8ddfe65","type":"text","text":"# `Response`\nRepresents an HTTP response issued from the server to an active connection\n\n## Types\n\n## Member Functions\n- `serialize`: Returns a string (or some other form) to send through the socket directly as the raw response\n## Data Members\n- An int for the HTTP status code\n- A string for the entire response string (`200 OK` for example)","x":3040,"y":-4440,"width":937,"height":1600},
		{"id":"6a5e75190ad0c3a1","type":"text","text":"# Connection Manager\n**The main event loop**. Essentially, it's here for routing requests and responses to clients.\nCould be part of the main function directly or executed as part of another function.\n> `socket`, `send`, `recv`, `bind`, `connect`, `listen`, `accept`, `poll`, `epoll`, `kevent`, `htons`, `htonl`, `ntohs`, `noths`\n## Responsibilities\nThe `Connection` objects live here.\nReceives incoming connections and manages existing ones.\n`Listener` objects manage incoming connections and create `Connection` objects accordingly. These are stored in a map of listening socket to listener. Depending on which socket was contacted, it looks up the right `Listener` and asks it to create a new `Connection` using the new connection socket issued by the listener socket.\n### Calling Parsing\n`Connection` objects contain a `Request` being built/processed, and a `Response` being sent.\nAs data comes through sockets, the parser will have to be called every time **with that data chunk** and a reference to the incomplete `Request` object.\nThe parser will then set the request status indicating whether it needs more data from the client or if the `Request` is ready to be processed.\n### Processing Requests\nSends incoming requests to the Request Processor as a Request object.\nReceives the requested data back from the Request Processor and sends them to the relevant socket\n### Deleting Connections\nWhen a `Connection` object signals through its return status that it is ready to be closed, the main loop **removes its fd from the polled array and deletes the `Connection` object, in that order**.\n## Receiving/Sending Data\nThis is where all the sockets fun happens: `poll`, `accept` and `send`.\nThe loop polls all active sockets, as well as CGI fds to read the content from and send it to the client that requested it.\n### CGI\nWhen receiving an fd from the Request Processor for CGI, it stores it in a map from fd to `Connection` object.\nThis makes it easy to lookup which connection to send the data to when the fd becomes readable, and clearly marks which fds are for CGI and which are sockets.\n### Error Pages\nWhen the Request Processor (or some other error happens), the Connection Manager sends a pre-made error page, or checks the `error_page` directive to know where to pull the page from.","x":-1160,"y":-4452,"width":740,"height":1052,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"a3f2aa91b73d8bba","type":"text","text":"# Request Parser\nThe Connection Manager will give data from the socket to the request parser, which will store it in a `Request` instance.\nAs the data arrives, and the stages of parsing progress, the `Request` gets built up little by little.\nThe parser **returns the number of bytes read in order to update the read buffer index in `Connection`**.\n## Stages\nEach request being parsed needs to go through different stages (as chunks of bytes from `recv` come through): parsing the request line -> headers -> body -> request is done.\n## Preparing for Processing\nThe parser is also responsible for **making the request ready to be processed as is**.\nThat means defining which `Config_Location` is relevant, setting all the fields to be processed, so that the Request Processor can just work on it without doing further analysis.","x":-1060,"y":-3205,"width":560,"height":486,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"01bed0363669d98c","type":"text","text":"# Driver\nThis is the main function\n> `access`, `stat`, `open`, `close`\n## Responsibilities\n1. Do basic checks for config file access, then call the Parser.\n2. Create the Connection Manager with its individual Listeners (?)\n3. Start the Connection Manager event loop\n4. When the event loop stops, check for any errors (exception, return value) and clean up before exiting.","x":-1800,"y":-3314,"width":580,"height":595,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"e2202cc847dfb53f","type":"text","text":"# Configuration Parser\n## Responsibilities\nParses the configuration file once at the start of the program and stores the configuration for later use.\nWhen prompted, provides all configuration in nice array for all routes available in the config, as well as a global configuration\n","x":-1980,"y":-4452,"width":520,"height":514,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"6e9d8415dc547c2d","type":"text","text":"# `epoll` or `poll`?\n`epoll` is clearly the wayyy better option, but it comes with a caveat of not being portable outside of Linux.\nTo be honest, I think it's a good plan to use `epoll` instead of `poll`; we're not going to test webserv on macos anyways.\nAnd who even runs web servers on macos/windows nowadays?","x":-900,"y":-5065,"width":480,"height":360,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"f7783e0a9eb791d2","type":"text","text":"# Config File\n> Directives are divided into simple directives and block directives. A simple directive consists of the name and parameters separated by spaces and ends with a semicolon (`;`). A block directive has the same structure as a simple directive, but instead of the semicolon it ends with a set of additional instructions surrounded by braces (`{` and `}`). If a block directive can have other directives inside braces, it is called a context (examples: [events](https://nginx.org/en/docs/ngx_core_module.html#events), [http](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_core_module.html#http), [server](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_core_module.html#server), and [location](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_core_module.html#location)).\n> Directives placed in the configuration file outside of any contexts are considered to be in the [main](https://nginx.org/en/docs/ngx_core_module.html) context. The `events` and `http` directives reside in the `main` context, `server` in `http`, and `location` in `server`.\n> The rest of a line after the `#` sign is considered a comment.[^1]\n## Structure\nOur config file for webserv should be directly based on nginx's config file format. Each instruction must have the same name as in nginx if present, and the structure must match nginx's as closely as possible. Obviously **we won't implement every feature** of every instruction.\n**Optionals/nice to haves are in italic.**\n- *[`error_log`](https://nginx.org/en/docs/ngx_core_module.html#error_log)*: Defines a file to redirect all error messages to, with potentially log levels? Would need to see how that translates in code\n- [`server {}`](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_core_module.html#server)\n\t- [`listen`](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_core_module.html#listen): Defines the interface AND port to listen on\n\t- [`error_page`](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_core_module.html#error_page): Sets default error pages for given HTTP error codes\n\t- [`client_max_body_size`](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_core_module.html#client_max_body_size): Maximum allowed size for client request bodies in bytes\n\t- `timeout`: Time in seconds without activity before a connection is closed.\n\t- [`location {}`](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_core_module.html#location) (without any of the modifiers)\n\t\t- [`limit_except`](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_core_module.html#limit_except): Allow/deny specific HTTP methods for a location\n\t\t- [`redirect`](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_rewrite_module.html#return): Define HTTP redirections and status code for those redirections\n\t\t- [`root`](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_core_module.html#root): Defines the root of the location\n\t\t- [`autoindex`](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_autoindex_module.html#autoindex): Bool for enabling directory listings\n\t\t- [`index`](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_index_module.html#index): Default file to serve \n\t\t- `upload_store`: Enables uploads by its presence and defines where uploaded files should go (this is custom and not present in nginx)\n\t\t- `cgi <extension> <interpreter_path>`: (this is custom and not present in nginx)\n\n## Passing Config Around\nThere remains the problem of **how to give access to the relevant parts of the configuration to different modules**?\nGiving a pointer to some core struct is fine and dandy, but there has to be a better way.\nI think the right approach is to give the least amount of information each module has to work with.\nFor example, if you're trying to process a Request, you'd pass a reference to the request's specific location directive inside of the config to the request processor, who'd do the rest.\nThen, if the request processor itself needs to call cgi, the cgi module only needs the interpreter path, not the entire `Config_Location`.\n\n[^1]: https://nginx.org/en/docs/beginners_guide.html\n","x":-1920,"y":-5920,"width":848,"height":1235,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"b2389484bcca92d1","type":"text","text":"# Working Notes\n- I thought we would need pipelining (queuing requests, storing multiple pending requests) but the subject doesn't require it, and it's actually not even used in the real world (HTTP/2 multiplexing is used instead).\n  That's why we're only going to store one pending Request and Response per Connection.\n- We have a parser module that's separate from the `Request` class, but the serialization will be a member function of `Response`. This is because the latter is way simpler than parsing, that the former needs to keep a potential beginning of first request around in the `Connection` buffer.","x":3467,"y":-5616,"width":510,"height":416},
		{"id":"e6840baf7029c639","type":"text","text":"# `config.hpp`\nStores all relevant information retrieved from the configuration file on startup.\nSince we know the structure of the configuration at compile time, it would make sense to **have structs of structs with the exact fields we need**. Sure it's less easily extensible, but it's still very sensible and used by a lot of projects, including nginx itself.\nFor directives that can have multiples instances, like servers and locations, **vectors will do fine**.\n# Structure of the Config\nI'd imagine the configuration to be represented in code as a class `Config` which stores all global configuration, as well as individual, route configurations (directory, redirection, methods, etc...)\nFor nginx, there are **servers** that contain **locations**. The servers define **the port it will listen on**, and the locations **where to fetch files on the host based on the URI**.\nI'm going for **structs** right now, but of course that would be easily convertible to classes should we need actual member functions. Structs are just classes, after all.\n## Types\nWe'll need multiple structs: `Config`, `Config_Server`, `Config_Location`, etc.\nOne for each of the block directives in the configuration, really. Keeping it simple with a lot of separate types.\n### `Config`\nStores all root directives, including the `http {}` directive.\n### `Config_Server`\nStores listening interface and port, default error pages, client_max_body_size int, a vector of `Location_Config`.\n### `Config_Location`\nHttp redirection information, root, autoindex, index, upload_store as well as cgi information and allowed http methods.","x":4040,"y":-4440,"width":1220,"height":1600},
		{"id":"af0d87420fe337bf","type":"text","text":"# `Connection`\nRepresents an active connection from a client.\n## RAII\n`Connection` objects are owners of their **socket**, which means they are responsible for closing it in the destructor.\nThe Connection Manager will be responsible for deleting `Connections`, which means it mustn't forget about removing the socket the `Connection` holds from its polled fds.\nThis must occur **before destroying the connection (otherwise we get a nasty race condition if a new connection is being created at the same time, getting the same fd number)**.\n## Member Functions\n- Getters for both the `Request` and `Response`\n- A function to send a chunk of data from the `Response`\n- A function to receive a chunk of data and give it to the request parser to store inside of the `Request`\n- A setter for the CGI file descriptor, so that the routing can know where to send that fd's data.\n- A timestamp to the last time we received data through the socket, in order to know when to time out the connection.\nMember functions that send and receive can signal through their return value that the `Connection` object is ready to be closed.\nThis way, the main loop can remove the right fd from the polled fds and delete the `Connection`.\n## Data Members\n- A reference? Pointer? Just the number? to the active connection socket through which requests will be received.\n- A read buffer to read from the socket into. This is where requests will live before being parsed, and where there might be the start of the next request if you finished parsing the first one.\n- An index indicating where the unprocessed data starts. This prevents having to remove characters from the buffer at every parsing iteration, instead clearing that data as each request has finished being parsed.\n  This takes up more memory but significantly lowers the time needed to parse requests.\n- A boolean indicating whether the socket is ready to be read from. This allows us to only read a little on epoll events, and then keep track that we still have to read from it some more without having to wait for another epoll event.\n  Otherwise, this causes us to wait for something that has already happened.\n- A write buffer, to store pending data to be sent (if the response is too long to be sent in one go).\n- The currently pending `Request`; built in-place by the parser and then processed by the Request Processor, probably by giving it a reference.\n- The currently pending `Response` object to be sent through the socket.\n- A const reference to the relevant `Config_Server` object.","x":2060,"y":-4440,"width":900,"height":1600},
		{"id":"02c2072a17f2e4f4","type":"text","text":"# `Request`\nRepresents a request issued by an active connection.\nAs it gets built by the Parser, its data members will slowly get populated.\n## Types\n- `http_method`: An enum denoting the HTTP method the request used.\n- `parsing_status`: An enum defining the status of the request\n  `EMPTY`, `REQUEST_LINE`, `HEADERS`, `BODY`, `PARSED`\n  A request is ready to be processed only when the `PARSED` status has been reached.\n## Member Functions\n\n## Data Members\n- An enum representing the request method.\n- A status enum, indicating the parsing status. This is set by the Parser as it progresses.\n- A const reference to the relevant `Config_Location` of the configuration.","x":1120,"y":-4440,"width":860,"height":1600},
		{"id":"244b7e4837cb2d96","type":"file","file":"Computer Science/Networking/Common Gateway Interface.md","x":-100,"y":-2051,"width":940,"height":1304},
		{"id":"2ad02c6a646a8021","type":"text","text":"# Storing files\nThe subject mentions configuring the storage locations for files clients might send. For nginx, this is actually handled by CGI scripts in the backend; but for us, this is going to be handled by the server itself.","x":-320,"y":-2520,"width":440,"height":321,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"8653170cc585fd0e","type":"file","file":"Computer Science/Networking/HyperText Transfer Protocol.md","x":-1123,"y":-2059,"width":963,"height":1312},
		{"id":"77a7d841da8ee77f","type":"text","text":"\n# Resources\n## RFCs\n- RFC [1945](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1945) - The HTTP 1.0 standard, for the core functionality of HTTP, the *how it works*, not *what it means* (the semantics).\n- RFC [9112](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9112) - The HTTP 1.1 standard, for when we need to implement 1.1 specific features.\n- RFC [9110](https://www.rfcreader.com/#rfc9110) - Provides a more recent covering of all HTTP semantics, and as such **obsoletes a whole lot of older RFCs, including the 72XX ones**.\n  Think of it as *the glossary of HTTP*.\n- RFC [3875](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3875) - The [[Common Gateway Interface]] specification.\n## Other Resources\n- [A Simple HTTP Server from Scratch](https://trungams.github.io/2020-08-23-a-simple-http-server-from-scratch/) - Example of a basic web server using sockets and c++ 98\n- [Exploring nginx's structure](https://aosabook.org/en/v2/nginx.html) - Chapter of a book about open source applications\n  Useful to understand of nginx works inside","x":-1880,"y":-2002,"width":620,"height":562},
		{"id":"26d337a6c3ac2719","type":"text","text":"# Subject PDF notes\n- The requirement about not checking `errno` after read/write operations probably relates to the fact that `-1` as a return value **doesn't always mean an error** if errno is `EAGAIN` or `EWOULDBLOCK`.\n- `poll` or equivalent means: `poll`, `epoll`, `select`\n- `epoll` is basically `poll` but better and least portable; might be useful if we make our webserv only usable on Linux, which I think we should but is up to debate\n- Obviously we only have to use non-blocking IO in the case of network stuff, local read/writes are exempt\n","x":-1880,"y":-1399,"width":624,"height":652},
		{"id":"359680b1fa8c1bac","type":"text","text":"# CGI\nGenerates content dynamically based on the standardized request passed by the Request Processor.\n> `fork`, `execve`, `pipe`, `dup`, `dup2`\n## Responsibilities\nForks the right CGI script based on the request, and sends back the read end of the pipe for the generated content.\nAll subprocess magic must happen here.\nYou pass down HTTP headers and other relevant information to the CGI script using environment variables.\nThe CGI script would give the content back through a pipe.\n\n> python and ruby","x":360,"y":-3187,"width":460,"height":470,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"1056644817197069","type":"text","text":"# Request Processor\nEach `Request` object has to keep track of its current state; has it been processed, sent etc. yet?\nThe Request processor uses the configuration stored in the `Config_Location` reference of the `Request` object to decide routing of requests (where the URIs point to, etc.)\n## Responsibilities\n### Path Normalization and Sanitization\nThe Request Processor gets the full path of the resource that's needed by using the URI from the Request, as well as the `root` configuration.\nIt normalizes the path (taking care of stuff like `.`, `..`, redundant slashes, etc.) and checks whether the file exists and, especially, can be accessed or not (careful about symlinks!).\nThis is done with a stack, each folder is pushed on a stack and each `..` pops a folder. At the end, check that the final path is still under the location root, otherwise, `403`.\n### Getting/Storing/Deleting Resources\nBased on the HTTP method, we'll get, post (process/store) or delete resources.","x":220,"y":-4121,"width":560,"height":640,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"7030fb2be992c307","type":"text","text":"# File Manager\nAll file-related functions are managed here (except CGI).\n> `opendir`, `readdir`, `closedir`, `open`, `close`, `read`, `write`, `access`, `stat`\n## Responsibilities\n1. Get files for the Request Processor or the Connection Manager\n2. Store files of POST requests\n3. Create directory listings when Request Processor asks for it\n4. In any case, return a string containing the resource that was asked for","x":-380,"y":-3205,"width":540,"height":391,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"5f94a73fae40d947","type":"link","url":"https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/epoll.7.html","x":880,"y":-2059,"width":760,"height":1304},
		{"id":"58116d1738ff7f2f","type":"text","text":"# Timing Connections Out\nThe Connection Manager will be responsible to check whether connections have timed out, and closing them if required (remember, take the fd off the epoll array and then destroy the connection object).\nWe'll have to calculate a timeout value for the connection that was active the longest ago, get the difference with the max timeout for that connection, and give that to `epoll_wait`.\nThat way, if `epoll_wait` times out, we know that this connection has to be closed.\nIf the difference between time elapsed and timeout value is less or equal to 0, close the connection immediately.","x":-250,"y":-5148,"width":570,"height":377,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"b1b1ac90e8a0dd78","type":"text","text":"# On Exceptions\nExceptions thrown by CGI or the File Manager would contain an HTTP status code and the reason for the exception.","x":420,"y":-4771,"width":360,"height":178,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"f6f3a418cb075f22","x":-1118,"y":-2576,"width":558,"height":436,"color":"5","type":"text","text":"# Parsing HTTP Requests\n1. Be lenient on terminators (`\\n` instead of `\\r\\n`)\n2. Be strict on separators (if a single space must be present between tokens, then don't accept anything else)\n3. Take care of chunked request though the `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` header\n4. If neither chunked encoding or a `Content-Length` header is set, assume no body."}
	],
